## Working at Better

Software engineering is more than writing good code. Ultimately, software must help a company achieve its goals and generate real business value. When engineers understand the business, they ask "why" more often, challenge requirements when needed, and make better product decisions based on data.

Being a **product-minded engineer** means:

1. Suggesting ideas and improvements, not just following specifications.
2. Understanding business goals, user behavior, and relevant data.
3. Being curious and wanting to understand the “why.”
4. Communicating well and building trust with non-engineers.
5. Considering both product and engineering trade-offs.
6. Handling edge cases practically without unnecessary work.
7. Seeking early feedback to validate features.
8. Taking end-to-end ownership and caring about real-world impact.

**Example:**  
If 60% of users have small screens while developers use 4K monitors, UI decisions may not reflect real user experience. Knowing your audience avoids wasted work.

Engineers should regularly review user analytics and error reports to better understand how their product is being used.

